@*
    A form control that lives in a table cell rather than in a form grid.

    A `<th scope="col">` names a *cell*; it never names a control inside one. An
    editable grid — the operations editor is the app's example — therefore ships
    rows of inputs with no accessible name at all: "edit, blank", "spin button,
    15", "spin button, 0.8". Wrapping the control in a label whose text is visually
    hidden gives it the same accessible name every other field in the app has,
    without adding a second visible column heading to each row.

    The name has to identify the row as well as the column, because a keyboard user
    who tabs into the middle of a twelve-operation routing has no other clue which
    operation they are editing. Compose it as "<column> — <row>", e.g.
    `Label="@($"{L["Field_SetupTime"]} — {L["Field_OperationNo"]} {op.OperationNumber}")"`.
*@
<label class="cell-field">
    <span class="sr-only">@Label</span>
    @ChildContent
</label>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The control's accessible name. Name the column and the row, in that order:
    /// the column is what the user is looking for, the row is what disambiguates it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = "";

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
